" (https://www.joppebos.com/montgomery/book_ch1.pdf)\nciteturn4view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 18; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.joppebos.com/montgomery/book_ch1.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 433\nL0@P0: Introduction to\nL1@P0: Topics in computational number theory\nL2@P0: inspired by Peter L. Montgomery\nL3@P0: Joppe W. Bos, Arjen K. Lenstra, Herman te Riele, and Daniel Shumow\nL4@P0: NXP Semiconductors, Leuven, Belgium\nL5@P0: EPFL, Lausanne, Switzerland\nL6@P0: CWI, Amsterdam, Netherlands\nL7@P0: Microsoft Research, Redmond, USA\nL8@P0: Abstract\nL9@P0: This article appeared as the introductory chapter of the book Topics in Compu\u0002tational Number Theory inspired by Peter L. Montgomery, edited by Joppe W.\nL10@P0: Bos and Arjen K. Lenstra and published by Cambridge University Press. See\nL11@P0: www.cambridge.org/9781107109353. It contains multiple cross-references\nL12@P0-1: to the other chapters of the same book.Contents\nL13@P1: 1 Introduction page 1\nL14@P1: 1.1 Outline 1\nL15@P1: 1.2 Biographical sketch 1\nL16@P1: 1.3 Overview 5\nL17@P1: 1.4 Simultaneous inversion 8\nL18@P1: Bibliography 11\nL19@P1-3: Subject index 151\nL20@P3: Introduction\nL21@P3: Joppe W. Bos, Arjen K. Lenstra, Herman te Riele and Daniel Shumow\nL22@P3: 1.1 Outline\nL23@P3: This introductory chapter collects some personal background on Peter L. Mont\u0002gomery, both provided by himself and based on stories from his colleagues and\nL24@P3: collaborators. This is followed by a brief introduction to the other chapters, and\nL25@P3: by a description of Peter’s simultaneous inversion method.\nL26@P3: 1.2 Biographical sketch\nL27@P3: Peter was born in San Francisco, California, on September 25, 1947. He de\u0002scribed his activities since his 1965 graduation from San Rafael High School\nL28@P3: (SRHS) in a letter for a 1995 high school reunion; this letter is copied in its\nL29@P3: entirety in Figure 1.1, and further commented on throughout this chapter.\nL30@P3: At Berkeley, Peter’s undergraduate advisor was Derrick H. Lehmer, an ex\u0002cellent match given Peter’s research interests since high school. Ron Gra\u0002ham, one of Peter’s coauthors on his first three papers (all on Ramsey theory\nL31@P3: and written shortly after his time in Berkeley), recalls [25] that Peter had the\nL32@P3: “breakthrough idea” for the first of these papers, with coauthor Bruce Roth\u0002schild remembering Peter “as an extremely clever guy who came up with sur\u0002prising ideas” [51]. The papers in question ([22], [23] and [24]) were further\nL33@P3: coauthored by Paul Erdös, Joel Spencer, and Ernst Straus. After this auspi\u0002cious start, Peter’s next paper describes an algorithm that he developed at SDC\nL34@P3: to evaluate arbitrary Fortran boolean expressions in such a way that they re\u0002sult in a small number of instructions when compiled on a Control Data 7600\nL35@P3: computer [35]. The paper is illustrative of the type of tinkering in which Peter\nL36@P3: excels.\nL37@P3-4: About twenty years after his time in Berkeley, and by the time that Pe-2\nL38@P4: Figure 1.1 Peter’s letter for his 1995 high-school reunion\nL39@P4: ter was already “internationally famous” (cf. Figure 1.1), David G. Cantor\nL40@P4: supervised Peter’s PhD dissertation An FFT extension of the elliptic curve\nL41@P4: method of factorization [38]. Milos Ercegovac, who served on the PhD com\u0002mittee, related [21] that Cantor referred to Peter as “a genius UCLA never\nL42@P4: saw before”, how Peter was brilliant in solving engineering problems related\nL43@P4-5: to computer arithmetic, and that, as a result of his incisive reviews, authorsIntroduction 3\nL44@P5: (a) At age 15 (b) In 2009\nL45@P5: Figure 1.2 Peter L. Montgomery (from Peter’s collection and Wikipedia)\nL46@P5: on several occasions asked if they could include this “marvelous” reviewer\nL47@P5: as a coauthor because Peter’s comments invariably provided better solutions\nL48@P5: than those given in the submissions. Another member of the committee, Mur\u0002ray Schacher, recalled [52] how Peter “regularly swamped the system” in what\nL49@P5: may have been his first venture into large scale computing: verifying reducibil\u0002ity of X\nL50@P5: 8k + Xm + 1 ∈ F2[X] for large k and 0 < m < 8k. For this project Peter\nL51@P5: taught himself the Cantor-Zassenhaus method because the non-sparse 8k × 8k\nL52@P5: matrix required by the usual Berlekamp algorithm surpassed the system’s ca\u0002pacity [4, 11].\nL53@P5: Peter did not accept the offer for a “research position in a New Jersey com\u0002munity lacking sidewalks” (cf. Figure 1.1). The offer was made after Peter’s\nL54@P5: successful visits at the Bellcore research facility in Morristown, NJ, during the\nL55@P5: summers of 1994 and 1995. During his stays Peter worked with the second\nL56@P5: author’s summer internship students Scott Contini and Phong Nguyen on im\u0002plementing Peter’s new methods that were useful for the number field sieve\nL57@P5: integer factorization algorithm (cf. Section 1.3 below): block Lanczos with\nL58@P5: Scott in 1994 and the next year the final square root method with Phong (who\nL59@P5: for his project had to learn about integer lattices and lattice basis reduction,\nL60@P5: and did so only very reluctantly – after a scolding by Ramarathnam “Venkie”\nL61@P5: Venkatesan). A memorable event was a lab outing where Peter beat Phong at\nL62@P5-6: bowling and afterwards bought ice cream for the whole party.4\nL63@P6: Figure 1.3 Front cover of [3] (© System Development Corporation, 1981).\nL64@P6: In 1998 Peter accepted a position with the Security and Cryptography group\nL65@P6: at Microsoft Research (MSR) in Redmond, WA. He retired in 2014. His work\nL66@P6: at MSR was largely focused on pure research (cf. [30], [16], [41], [19], [20],\nL67@P6: and [9]). However, Peter began implementing libraries for asymmetric en\u0002cryption for use in products as well. Peter’s first contribution was to write\nL68@P6: Diffie–Hellman key exchange and the Digital Signature Algorithm in the static\nL69@P6: bignum library. This was included in the default Microsoft Windows CAPI 1.0\nL70@P6: library dssenh.dll, that shipped in Windows 2000. A later version of this library,\nL71@P6: msbignum, also included RSA as well as elliptic curve cryptography. In 2006,\nL72@P6: with Windows Vista, msbignum became the basis of the API for the Crypt\u0002ographic Next Generation and underlied in the following decade all crypto\u0002graphy running on Windows at Microsoft. Peter’s role may have been unique\nL73@P6: at MSR at that time: over the course of the organization’s existence there have\nL74@P6: been very few teams at MSR, let alone individual researchers, delivering entire\nL75@P6: functional software libraries.\nL76@P6: During his time at MSR, Peter continued collaborations with researchers\nL77@P6: world-wide and was a regular long-term guest at various universities and re\u0002search institutes. The first three authors of this chapter have fond memories of\nL78@P6: Peter’s visits (and are not the only ones who have lugged around Peter’s con\u0002siderable suitcase when regular cabs were not up to the task [25]) – as Milos\nL79@P6: Ercegovac put it: a modest man with a sense of humor, a gentle chuckle when\nL80@P6: people make silly technical comments [21]. Numerous papers were the result\nL81@P6: of his visits: several record factorizations (140 digits [14], 512 bits [15], 768\nL82@P6: bits [27, 28], and Mersenne numbers [8]); security estimates [5]; elliptic curve\nL83@P6: discrete logarithm cryptanalysis using Playstation 3 game consoles [7, 6]; im\u0002proved stage two for Pollard’s p±1 method [42]; better elliptic curve factoring\nL84@P6-7: parameter selection [2]; and results on the period of Bell numbers [43].Introduction 5\nL85@P7: While discussing his work at SDC in the early software industry Peter liked\nL86@P7: to proudly show a copy of “The System Builders: The Story of SDC” [3] with\nL87@P7: his name displayed on the front cover, about an inch underneath the title (cf.\nL88@P7: Figure 1.3). Peter never showed a similar pride in his more celebrated algo\u0002rithmic and mathematical results – possibly because his work as a software\nL89@P7: engineer greatly motivated his mathematical research and he invented new ap\u0002proaches just to make his implementations more efficient. This is illustrated by\nL90@P7: Peter’s explanation how he got the inspiration for his paper Modular multiplic\u0002ation without trial division [36], arguably one of his most widely-used results:\nL91@P7: when implementing textbook multiprecision multiplication in assembler on a\nL92@P7: PDP series computer, he noticed there were unused registers and wanted to find\nL93@P7: a way to exploit them. He managed to do so by interleaving the multiplication\nL94@P7: with the modular reduction.\nL95@P7: When Peter began working, at SDC, he programmed using punch cards on\nL96@P7: time-shared main frame computers. By the time he had retired from MSR,\nL97@P7: he had implemented and optimized arithmetic code that was running on the\nL98@P7: mobile processors in smartphones. Peter worked as a programmer from the\nL99@P7: earliest stages of software engineering when computers needed to be held in\nL100@P7: their own buildings until the era of ubiquitous computing where people carry\nL101@P7: computing devices in their pockets. Both his algorithmic and mathematical\nL102@P7: contributions and his work as a programmer were instrumental to these massive\nL103@P7: advances that spanned his career – a career of more than forty years, impressive\nL104@P7: by the standards of the software industry.\nL105@P7: 1.3 Overview\nL106@P7: The subsequent chapters of this book are on active research areas that were ini\u0002tiated by Peter or to which he has made substantial contributions. This section\nL107@P7: contains an overview of the subjects that are treated.\nL108@P7: As mentioned above, Peter’s 1985 method for modular multiplication with\u0002out trial division [36] was invented almost serendipitously when Peter tried to\nL109@P7: put otherwise idle registers to good use. Because it allows simple implement\u0002ation both in software and in hardware, Montgomery multiplication (as the\nL110@P7: method is now commonly referred to) has found broad application. Its soft\u0002ware and hardware aspects are covered in chapters 2 and 3, respectively.\nL111@P7: Peter’s contributions as described and expanded upon in chapters 4 through 8\nL112@P7: were all inspired by integer factorization, his main research interest since high\nL113@P7: school. In [37, Section 10.3.1] Peter describes how his original implementation\nL114@P7-8: of the elliptic curve method of integer factorization [33] uses the Weierstrass6\nL115@P8: equation with affine coordinates, and how the modular inversion required for\nL116@P8: the point addition can be made faster if several curves are run at once (cf. Sec\u0002tion 1.4 on page 8). He then writes “The author later discovered an alternative\nL117@P8: parametrization that requires no inversions” and continues with the description\nL118@P8: of what is now known as Montgomery curves and the Montgomery ladder.\nL119@P8: The former is an elliptic curve parametrization that allows fast computation of\nL120@P8: the sum of two points if their difference is known and that does not require\nL121@P8: modular inversion. The latter refers to the addition chain that can then be used\nL122@P8: to compute any scalar multiple of a point. Initially these results were relevant\nL123@P8: solely to those implementing the elliptic curve integer factorization method,\nL124@P8: but the later growing importance of elliptic curve cryptography increased the\nL125@P8: interest in Peter’s and yet other curve parametrizations and addition chains.\nL126@P8: Chapter 4 describes the developments in this field that have taken place since\nL127@P8: the publication of [37].\nL128@P8: In parallel with his work on special purpose integer factoring methods in\nL129@P8: [37], Peter also heavily influenced the two most important methods of the\nL130@P8: more generic integer factorization algorithms that are described in Chapter 5:\nL131@P8: the quadratic sieve and the number field sieve, two methods that were ini\u0002tially plagued by severe performance problems [48, 31]. Although Peter was\nL132@P8: not the first to propose a practical version of the quadratic sieve, his multi\u0002ple polynomial quadratic sieve (as published by Robert Silverman in [54] and\nL133@P8: described in Chapter 5), represented the state of the art in integer factoriza\u0002tion from the mid 1980s until the early 1990s. It was later enhanced by self\u0002initialization [50], but by that time the number field sieve had taken over as the\nL134@P8: record-breaking factoring method, a position it holds to the present day. Peter\nL135@P8: played a prominent role in that development, contributing important ideas to\nL136@P8: all steps of the number field sieve: polynomial selection, (early) sieving, build\u0002ing a matrix, processing the matrix, and the final square root calculation. A\nL137@P8: brief summary follows, with Chapter 5 describing in more detail how the steps\nL138@P8: fit together.\nL139@P8: In the first step of the number field sieve two or more polynomials must be\nL140@P8: found that have a root in common modulo the number to be factored. Though\nL141@P8: theoretically such polynomials are easily found, small modifications may trig\u0002ger orders of magnitude reductions of the resulting factoring effort. Finding\nL142@P8: good polynomials has become a subject in its own right since Peter’s early\nL143@P8: contributions (many of which were first published in Brian Murphy’s 1999\nL144@P8: PhD dissertation [45]). Chapter 6 is entirely devoted to the current state of the\nL145@P8: art of polynomial selection for the number field sieve.\nL146@P8: The number field sieve (and many other sieving-based integer factorization\nL147@P8-9: methods) relies on the ability to quickly recognize which values in an arith-Introduction 7\nL148@P9: metic progression have many small factors. Chapter 5 briefly mentions some\nL149@P9: of Peter’s many tricks which are now part of the standard repertoire to speed up\nL150@P9: this so-called sieving step. His improvements to the earliest line sieving soft\u0002ware for the number field sieve by the second author were used for numerous\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn4view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.ams.org/mcom/1987-48-177/S0025-5718-1987-0866113-7/S0025-5718-1987-0866113-7.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://www.ams.org/mcom/1987-48-177/S0025-5718-1987-0866113-7/S0025-5718-1987-0866113-7.pdf: (403) Forbidden--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGPU-Accelerated Big Integer Arithmetic and Number Theoretic Transform for Cryptography in NVIDIA cuPQC 0.6 - cuPQC SDK Developer Hub (https://nvidia.github.io/cuPQC/releases/v0.6/)\nciteturn4view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://nvidia.github.io/cuPQC/releases/v0.6/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 795\nL0: [Input] [Input]\nL1: \nL2: 【0†Skip to content】\nL3: \nL4: 【1†Image: NVIDIA】 【2†Image: NVIDIA】 cuPQC SDK Developer Hub\nL5: \nL6: 【3†Home】 【4†Get Started】 【5†Applications】 【6†Blog】 【7†Docs】\nL7: \nL8: [Input] [Input]\nL9: \nL10: 【8†SDK v0.6.0†developer.nvidia.com】 【9†Sample Applications†github.com】\nL11: \nL12: [Input] 【3†Home】 【4†Get Started】 【5†Applications】 【6†Blog】 【7†Docs】\nL13: \nL14: 【8†SDK v0.6.0†developer.nvidia.com】 【9†Sample Applications†github.com】\nL15: \nL16: Categories\nL17: \nL18: 【6†All posts7】 【10†Release Blog3】 【11†Technical Blog2】 【12†Ecosystem2】\nL19: \nL20: Archive\nL21: 【6†All years7】 【13†20265】 【14†20251】 【15†20241】\nL22: # GPU-Accelerated Big Integer Arithmetic and Number Theoretic Transform for Cryptography in NVIDIA cuPQC 0.6\nL23: \nL24: August 28, 2026 cuPQC SDK v0.6\nL25: We're excited to announce cuPQC 0.6, delivering Big Integer Arithmetic (BigInt) and the Number Theoretic Transform (NTT) as foundational math layer primitives specifically built for cryptographic application developers working on NVIDIA GPUs. These libraries are engineered to power the entire cryptographic spectrum, from classical protocols and post-quantum cryptography (PQC) to the massive computational workloads at the core of Zero-Knowledge Proofs (ZKPs) and Fully Homomorphic Encryption (FHE).\nL26: ## What's New in 0.6\nL27: Whether you are scaling traditional public-key cryptography like RSA and ECC, implementing post-quantum schemes, or bringing advanced privacy-preserving technologies like ZKPs and FHE to production, you face the same bottleneck: executing millions of multi-precision integer operations and polynomial multiplications at high throughput.\nL28: Historically, accelerating these workloads on GPUs meant implementing and optimizing custom CUDA kernels from scratch for every specific ring dimension, bit width, and parameter set your schemes require.\nL29: cuPQC 0.6 eliminates this barrier by providing that arithmetic through the cuPQC-BigInt and cuPQC-NTT libraries as ready-to-use GPU primitives. You can now compose complex cryptographic protocols using accelerated multi-precision integer arithmetic and polynomial multiplication as standard building blocks.\nL30: These libraries are designed as device extensions, enabling you to fuse these arithmetic operations with your own specialized functions into a single kernel. This avoids unnecessary host-device data transfers and intermediate memory allocations, a critical requirement for applications like ZKP proving, FHE ciphertext evaluation, and large-scale public-key infrastructure.\nL31: Batch-oriented APIs enable thousands of concurrent operations to execute in parallel, letting you build faster, more scalable cryptographic solutions while focusing on your protocol design rather than arithmetic implementation.\nL32: This release adds:\nL33:   * cuPQC-BigInt: Provides fixed-width unsigned integers with full arithmetic up to 4096 bits and storage up to 8192 bits, with thread and warp execution models, Barrett and Montgomery reduction paths, and compile-time error-handling policies. It serves as the workhorse for classical cryptography, the classical halves of hybrid PQC protocols, and large-field ZKP arithmetic.\nL34:   * cuPQC-NTT: Provides cyclic forward and inverse transforms in the Montgomery domain, in both standard and staged execution modes, for transform lengths up to 2^{24} points over a user-selected prime up to 62 bits. This provides the exact heavy lifting required for the massive polynomial rings used in FHE and ZKP systems.\nL35: In our benchmarks, cuPQC-NTT runs 32-bit transforms 12× to 42× faster than a 28-core CPU across sizes from 2^{8} to 2^{24} points, and cuPQC-BigInt runs 34× to 773× faster than a single CPU core running GMP across the eight operations we measured.\nL36: These additions expand the cuPQC SDK into four libraries across two tiers. At the math layer, cuPQC-BigInt and cuPQC-NTT provide low-level arithmetic, while cuPQC-Hash delivers key ZKP primitives like Poseidon2 and Merkle trees. Together, they give developers full flexibility to either deploy ready-to-use algorithms or construct custom, high-performance cryptographic pipelines on GPUs.\nL37: The following sections walk through each library in detail, covering supported operations, key configuration parameters, and benchmark results.\nL38: ## cuPQC-BigInt: Multi-Precision Integer Arithmetic\nL39: Modern cryptographic algorithms work with numbers that are orders of magnitude larger than what standard processor types can handle natively. Zero-Knowledge Proofs operate over 256- to 384-bit scalar fields, ECC works over 256- to 512-bit prime fields, post-quantum isogeny schemes use primes in the 400- to 700-bit range, and traditional RSA keys span up to 4096 bits.\nL40: In implementations, these values are held in multi-precision representations, with modular arithmetic applied at full width throughout the protocol. When thousands of these operations must run concurrently at high throughput, carrying the load on CPUs alone quickly becomes an expensive bottleneck.\nL41: cuPQC-BigInt provides these values as fixed-width unsigned integers built from 32-bit little-endian limbs. Widths are multiples of 32. Full arithmetic is available up to 4096 bits, and the double-width type used for products stores up to 8192 bits. This single library covers standard key sizes, elliptic curve fields, and large ZKP proof-system fields without requiring a separate implementation for each.\nL42: The specific widths you can instantiate depend on how many threads cooperate on a single integer, as detailed in Table 2. Just like the native integer types they extend, arithmetic wraps modulo 2^{bit_width}.\nL43: A full product of two N-bit values is up to 2N bits, so `mul_wide` and `square` return a separate double-width type carrying twice the limbs, complete with `lo` and `hi` accessors for its halves. At the 4096-bit arithmetic limit that product is 8192 bits. That wide value is also what Barrett and Montgomery reductions take as input to reduce it back to a single width.\nL44: When you only need part of a product, `mul_low` and `mul_high` return a single width directly, and `mul_low` costs roughly half as much as a full wide multiply.\nL45: ### Operations\nL46: \nL47: The table below is a high-level view of the functions and operators. Overloads, execution-model restrictions, and full signatures are in 【16†Supported Operations†docs.nvidia.com】 in the cuPQC-BigInt Features guide.\nL48: \nL49: cuPQC-BigInt Operations\nL50: \nL51: `name()` = callable · `+` = operator\nL52: \nL53: Capability Functions and operators\nL54: \nL55:   * 01\nL56: \nL57: Construction and access\nL58: \nL59: Build a value from scalars or limbs, read parts of it back, and write it to global memory.\nL60: \nL61: `bigint()`from a 32- or 64-bit scalar, or a limb array\nL62: `store()`whole value, or one instance of a batch\nL63: \nL64: `to_uint32()``to_uint64()`low bits of the value\nL65: \nL66: `[]`one limb, least significant first; thread execution only\nL67: \nL68:   * 02\nL69: \nL70: Addition and subtraction\nL71: \nL72: Bigint and scalar operands; results wrap at the declared bit width.\nL73: \nL74: `add_scalar()``sub_scalar()`\nL75: \nL76: `+``-``+=``-=`\nL77: \nL78:   * 03\nL79: \nL80: Multiplication\nL81: \nL82: Low, high, and full products, and squares.\nL83: \nL84: `mul_low()``mul_high()``mul_scalar()``*=`single width, wraps at the declared bit width\nL85: `mul_wide()``square()``*`double-width result, with `lo` and `hi` halves\nL86: \nL87:   * 04\nL88: \nL89: Division and remainder\nL90: \nL91: Quotient and remainder, including from a double-width value.\nL92: \nL93: `div_rem()`returns a status; also on the double-width type\nL94: \nL95: `mod()``/``%`failures follow the on-error policy\nL96: \nL97:   * 05\nL98: \nL99: Comparison\nL100: \nL101: Ordering against another instance or a scalar.\nL102: \nL103: `compare()`three-way, against an instance or a scalar\nL104: \nL105: `==``!=``<``<=``>``>=`\nL106: \nL107:   * 06\nL108: \nL109: Bitwise and shifts\nL110: \nL111: Bit-level logic, shifts, and leading-zero count.\nL112: `&``|``^``~``&=``|=``^=`\nL113: \nL114: `<<``>>``<<=``>>=`\nL115: \nL116: `clz()`leading zeros over the full width\nL117: \nL118:   * 07\nL119: \nL120: Modular arithmetic\nL121: \nL122: Arithmetic under an arbitrary modulus.\nL123: \nL124: `add_mod()``sub_mod()``mul_mod()`\nL125: \nL126: `pow_mod()`scalar or big-integer exponent\nL127: \nL128: `inv_mod()`odd and even moduli\nL129: \nL130:   * 08\nL131: \nL132: Barrett reduction\nL133: \nL134: Precompute once against a fixed modulus, then reduce cheaply.\nL135: \nL136: `setup_barrett()`precompute against a fixed modulus\nL137: \nL138: `reduce_barrett()`reduce double-width values\nL139: \nL140:   * 09\nL141: \nL142: Montgomery arithmetic\nL143: Enter the Montgomery domain, multiply, and come back.\nL144: \nL145: `to_montgomery()``mul_montgomery()``from_montgomery()`\nL146: \nL147: `reduce_montgomery()`on double-width values\nL148: \nL149: Table 1. Device-side operations on the `bigint` and `bigint_wide` types\nL150: ### Execution Models\nL151: \nL152: The design decision that most affects performance is how a single integer maps onto CUDA threads. cuPQC-BigInt offers two execution models to balance latency and throughput:\nL153: Thread Execution (`Thread()`): Maps one integer to one thread. This provides maximum throughput when you have many independent values of moderate width. It is the ideal choice for batched elliptic-curve point arithmetic or ZKP field operations (such as BN254 or BLS12-381) running thousands of 256-bit operations side by side.\nL154: Warp Execution (`Warp()`): Splits one integer across multiple consecutive lanes, with carry propagation crossing lanes via warp intrinsics. You select this alongside a `TPI<N>()` parameter, where N is a power of two between 1 and 32. This model drastically reduces latency for massive integers. For example, a 3072-bit modular exponentiation spans 96 limbs; distributing it across 16 lanes shortens the dependent carry chain by a factor of 16.\nL155: Under warp execution, several instances can share a warp. `TPI<8>` packs four independent 8-lane instances into one 32-lane warp. Raising or lowering `TPI` trades per-integer latency against how many integers that warp computes, without rewriting the kernel.\nL156: \nL157: The lane count also dictates which bit widths you can build, as each lane requires enough limbs to justify its place in the warp.\nL158: \nL159: cuPQC-BigInt Supported Widths\nL160: \nL161: Every column past the first also takes `Warp()`\nL162: \nL163: 1 lane\nL164: \nL165: `Thread()`\nL166: `32 · 64 · 128``256-1024`\nL167: \nL168: 2 lanes\nL169: \nL170: `TPI<2>()`\nL171: \nL172: `128-2048`\nL173: \nL174: 4 lanes\nL175: \nL176: `TPI<4>()`\nL177: \nL178: `256-4096`\nL179: \nL180: 8 lanes\nL181: \nL182: `TPI<8>()`\nL183: \nL184: `512-4096`\nL185: \nL186: 16 lanes\nL187: \nL188: `TPI<16>()`\nL189: \nL190: `1024-4096`\nL191: \nL192: 32 lanes\nL193: \nL194: `TPI<32>()`\nL195: \nL196: `2048-4096`\nL197: \nL198: Table 2. Arithmetic bit widths built for each lane count\nL199: Every width inside a range is supported. For instance, 1184 bits across four lanes is a valid configuration. The compiler enforces two simple rules for these ranges: a warp configuration requires at least two limbs per lane, and maxes out at either 32 limbs per lane or 128 limbs total. Single-lane execution is the exception at the low end (32, 64, and 128 bits are valid, while 96, 160, 192, and 224 are not). Requesting a width outside these supported sets will satisfy the type system but fail to link.\nL200:     `#include <bigint.hpp>\nL201:     using namespace cupqc;\nL202: \nL203:     // One 1024-bit integer per thread.\nL204:     using BI1024 = decltype(BitWidth<1024>() + SM<800>() + Thread());\nL205: \nL206:     // One 3072-bit integer per 8 lanes; 4 instances per warp.\nL207:     using BI3072 = decltype(BitWidth<3072>() + SM<800>() + Warp() + TPI<8>());\nL208: \nL209:     __global__ void square_and_store(const uint32_t* in, uint32_t* out)\nL210:     {\nL211:         unsigned i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;\nL212:         BI1024::bigint a(in, i);      // load by instance index\nL213:         auto wide = a.square();       // double-width result\nL214:         wide.lo.store(out, i);        // store by instance index\nL215:     }\nL216:     `\nL217: ### Modular Reduction Paths\nL218: \nL219: Modular reduction is typically the heaviest computational step in any cryptographic protocol. There is no universal best algorithm for this step; the most efficient approach depends entirely on whether your protocol uses a modulus once or repeatedly in a loop. To maximize throughput, cuPQC-BigInt avoids a one-size-fits-all approach. Instead, it provides multiple explicit reduction paths, giving you the control to select the exact algorithm that fits your workload.\nL220: cuPQC-BigInt Modular Reduction Paths\nL221: \nL222: `name()` = callable · `%` = operator\nL223: \nL224: Capability Functions and operators Notes\nL225: \nL226:   * 01\nL227: \nL228: Add / subtract\nL229: \nL230: (a ± b) mod m\nL231: \nL232: `add_mod()``sub_mod()`\nL233: \nL234: Direct conditional reduction; no division. Requires a < m and b < m.\nL235: \nL236:   * 02\nL237: \nL238: General remainder\nL239: \nL240: a mod m\nL241: \nL242: `mod()``div_rem()``%`\nL243: \nL244: Uses the division core; no precomputation. Prefer `div_rem()` when you need a status code or quotient.\nL245: \nL246:   * 03\nL247: \nL248: General modular multiplication\nL249: \nL250: (a · b) mod m\nL251: \nL252: `mul_mod()`\nL253: Full wide multiply followed by division-based remainder; no precomputation.\nL254: \nL255:   * 04\nL256: \nL257: Repeated wide reduction\nL258: \nL259: same m\nL260: \nL261: `setup_barrett()``reduce_barrett()`\nL262: \nL263: Precompute once, then reduce multiple wide values (for example `mul_wide()` / `square()` results).\nL264: \nL265:   * 05\nL266: \nL267: Repeated multiplication\nL268: \nL269: same odd m\nL270: \nL271: `to_montgomery()``mul_montgomery()``from_montgomery()``reduce_montgomery()`\nL272: \nL273: Convert once and remain in Montgomery form across the operation chain.\nL274: \nL275:   * 06\nL276: \nL277: Exponentiate\nL278: \nL279: a^{e} mod m\nL280: \nL281: `pow_mod()`\nL282: Odd m uses Montgomery internally; even m uses `mul_mod()`. Not intended as a constant-time path for secret exponents.\nL283: 